description:
Build both halves of piggybacked acknowledgements (lesson 5.03): the receiver's `ack` and `ackBits`, and the sender's "which of my packets were delivered".

```cpp
class ReceivedPackets
{
public:
    void record(std::uint16_t sequence);
    std::uint16_t ack() const;
    std::uint32_t ackBits() const;
};

class SentPackets
{
public:
    void sent(std::uint16_t sequence);
    std::vector<std::uint16_t> onAck(std::uint16_t ack, std::uint32_t ackBits);
};
```

**ReceivedPackets.** Before anything is recorded, `ack` is 0 and `ackBits` is 0. The first recorded number becomes the ack with no bits. After that, with `d` the signed difference between the new number and the current ack (practice 5.02):

- `d > 0`: the new number becomes the ack; the old ack becomes bit `d - 1` and every old bit moves up by `d`; bits that would pass bit 31 are dropped;
- `d < 0`: set bit `-d - 1` if it is below 32, otherwise ignore the number;
- `d == 0`: nothing changes.

**SentPackets** remembers every sequence number passed to `sent` that has not yet been acknowledged. `onAck` looks at `ack` and at `ack - 1 - i` for each set bit `i`; each of those still waiting is now acknowledged and stops waiting. It returns them oldest first (the one furthest behind `ack` first, `ack` last), and a packet is never returned twice. Sending the same number again (after the counter wraps) makes it wait again.

